    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-locke">John Locke</a>, generally regarded as the father of modern liberalism, argued in his Two Treatises (1690) that lawful government rests on the consent of the governed, and that the people may overthrow a tyrant who violates life, liberty and property.</li><li>Leaders of the Glorious Revolution of 1688, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a> of 1776 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> of 1789 used liberal philosophy to justify the armed overthrow of royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>.</li><li>About 1900 the New Liberals argued that freedom needed a welfare-oriented state: in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> liberalism has since the 1930s usually meant this social liberalism, while in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> it often means classical liberalism of limited government.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The first group to use the label liberal in politics were the Spanish liberales, who fought for decades for the Spanish Constitution of 1812; the word liberalism first appeared in English in 1815.</p></aside>
    </section>

